.TH TEST 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
test, [ \- evaluate a conditional expression
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B test
.RI [ expression ]
.br
.B [
.RI [ expression ]
.B ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B test
evaluates
.I expression
and sets its own exit status according to the result (see
.B "EXIT STATUS"
below); it writes nothing to standard output.
.PP
When invoked as
.BR [ ,
the last argument must be a literal
.BR ] ,
which is not itself counted as part of
.IR expression ;
its absence is a diagnosed error. In this implementation the
.B [
spelling is available only as a shell built-in \(em there is no
standalone
.B [
executable installed alongside
.BR test .
.PP
Exactly how the arguments making up
.I expression
are interpreted depends on how many of them there are: an
argument-count-specific rule applies for zero, one, two, three, and
four arguments, and only expressions of five or more arguments are
evaluated by the general grammar described under
.B "COMPOUND EXPRESSIONS"
below.
.TP
.B "Zero arguments"
False.
.TP
.B "One argument"
True if the argument is not the empty string, false if it is.
.TP
.B "Two arguments"
If the first argument is
.BR ! ,
true if the second argument is the empty string, false otherwise
(this is a direct test of whether the second argument's own text is
empty, not a negation of some other test applied to it). Otherwise,
the first argument must be one of the unary primaries listed under
.B "UNARY PRIMARIES"
below, applied to the second argument; any other first argument is a
diagnosed error.
.TP
.B "Three arguments"
If the second argument is one of the binary primaries listed under
.B "BINARY PRIMARIES"
below, the binary test of the first and third arguments (this is
checked before anything else, which is why, for example,
.RB ` "test \(dq(\(dq = \(dq)\(dq" '
is a string comparison, not an attempt to parse a parenthesized
group). Otherwise, if the first argument is
.BR ! ,
the negation of the two-argument test of the remaining two arguments.
Otherwise, if the first and third arguments are
.B (
and
.BR ) ,
the one-argument test of the second. Any other combination is a
diagnosed error.
.TP
.B "Four arguments"
If the first argument is
.BR ! ,
the negation of the three-argument test of the remaining three
arguments. Otherwise, if the first and last arguments are
.B (
and
.BR ) ,
the two-argument test of the middle two arguments. Otherwise, the
expression is evaluated by the general grammar described below.
.TP
.B "Five or more arguments"
Evaluated by the general grammar described under
.B "COMPOUND EXPRESSIONS"
below.
.SH "UNARY PRIMARIES"
Each of these is written as the primary followed by one argument
(e.g.
.RB ` "\-f file" ').
.TP
.BI \-b " file"
True if
.I file
exists and is a block special file.
.TP
.BI \-c " file"
True if
.I file
exists and is a character special file.
.TP
.BI \-d " file"
True if
.I file
exists and is a directory.
.TP
.BI \-e " file"
True if
.I file
exists, regardless of its type.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
True if
.I file
exists and is a regular file.
.TP
.BI \-g " file"
True if
.I file
exists and its set-group-ID bit is set.
.TP
.BI \-h " file"
.TP
.BI \-L " file"
True if
.I file
exists and is itself a symbolic link (its own status is used, without
following the link); equivalent to each other.
.TP
.BI \-n " string"
True if the length of
.I string
is nonzero.
.TP
.BI \-p " file"
True if
.I file
exists and is a FIFO.
.TP
.BI \-r " file"
True if
.I file
exists and read permission is granted for it.
.TP
.BI \-S " file"
True if
.I file
exists and is a socket.
.TP
.BI \-s " file"
True if
.I file
exists and has a size greater than zero.
.TP
.BI \-t " fd"
True if
.I fd
is a syntactically valid, non-negative file descriptor number that is
currently open and associated with a terminal. Unlike most of the
other unary primaries above, an invalid or closed
.I fd
(including one that is not a valid non-negative decimal integer at
all) makes this false, not a diagnosed error.
.TP
.BI \-u " file"
True if
.I file
exists and its set-user-ID bit is set.
.TP
.BI \-w " file"
True if
.I file
exists and write permission is granted for it.
.TP
.BI \-x " file"
True if
.I file
exists and execute (or, for a directory, search) permission is granted
for it.
.TP
.BI \-z " string"
True if the length of
.I string
is zero.
.PP
.B \-a
as a
.I unary
primary (a synonym for
.B \-e
some other implementations provide) is deliberately not implemented,
to avoid ambiguity with the
.I binary
.B \-a
operator described below.
.SH "BINARY PRIMARIES"
Each of these is written between two arguments (e.g.
.RB ` "string1 = string2" ').
.TP
.I s1 " = " s2
True if the strings
.I s1
and
.I s2
are identical.
.TP
.I s1 " != " s2
True if the strings
.I s1
and
.I s2
are not identical.
.TP
.I n1 " \-eq " n2
.TP
.I n1 " \-ne " n2
.TP
.I n1 " \-lt " n2
.TP
.I n1 " \-le " n2
.TP
.I n1 " \-gt " n2
.TP
.I n1 " \-ge " n2
Numeric comparisons (equal, not equal, less than, less than or equal,
greater than, greater than or equal).
.I n1
and
.I n2
must each parse, in full, as a decimal integer (leading and trailing
blanks are tolerated); an operand that does not is a diagnosed error,
never treated as a false comparison.
.SH "COMPOUND EXPRESSIONS"
When the general grammar applies (five or more arguments, or the
four-argument fallback case above), primaries may be combined with
.B !
(negation, written before the primary it negates),
.B \-a
(logical AND), and
.B \-o
(logical OR), and grouped with
.BR "( expression )" .
Both
.B \-a
and
.B \-o
are left-associative, and
.B \-a
binds more tightly than
.BR \-o .
Both operands of
.B \-a
and of
.B \-o
are always evaluated, even when the left operand alone already
determines the result, so a malformed right operand is still a
diagnosed error rather than being silently skipped.
.PP
The
.B =
and
.B !=
binary primaries take precedence over any unary primary: a token
immediately followed by
.B =
or
.B !=
is always treated as the left operand of that comparison, never as a
unary primary's own operator.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.I expression
evaluated to true.
.TP
.B 1
.I expression
evaluated to false, or no expression was given at all.
.TP
.B >1
.I expression
was malformed (an unknown primary or operator, a missing argument, a
non-numeric operand to a numeric comparison, or, when invoked as
.BR [ ,
a missing closing
.BR ] ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR true (1),
.BR false (1)
